# Extension Invariants

Before changing extension runtime behavior, read the relevant current plan/specification.
The plan is the detailed source of truth; this rule only protects cross-cutting project decisions.

## Permissions and access

- Chrome-managed permissions are the source of truth for site access.
- Do not introduce a separate enabled/disabled-sites authority unless the current specification explicitly changes that design.
- Permission requests/removals that require user consent belong in the user-gesture path.
- Do not broaden extension permissions merely to make implementation easier.

## State

- Keep durable user preference, current tab behavior, and Chrome-managed permission state conceptually separate.
- Do not make storage writes part of the critical playback path.
- Do not let frame- or media-local behavior silently mutate tab-wide or persisted user intent.
- Persist unknown envelope and override-map fields; drop only malformed known fields.
- Extra keys inside a known Override object fail that field. Changing an existing field's representation requires a schemaVersion bump and a migrator (CURRENT_BEHAVIOR_SCHEMA_VERSION or CURRENT_THEME_SCHEMA_VERSION independently).
- Never rewrite, repair, evict-as-corrupt, or downgrade a settings record whose schemaVersion this build does not support.
- Destructive reset/delete of one logical record fails closed if any authoritative copy is unsupported or has opaque override extras this build cannot tombstone.

## Frames and content scripts

- Preserve the current top-frame/child-frame authority model defined by the plan.
- Do not let a child frame invent tab-wide state.
- Permission removal must reconcile already-running content scripts, not only future registrations.
- Treat opaque/fallback-frame behavior according to the current specification rather than guessing from `location.origin`.

## Messaging

- Parse incoming `unknown` at every runtime boundary. Privileged receivers
  (options/popup responses, storage) use regular Zod. Content-channel
  requests and background→content messages use Zod Mini in
  `protocol/content/**`. Background inbound parses content requests with
  those same Mini schemas and returns `{ channel, request }`.
- Keep RPC channel-bounded. Do not restore a global request union or
  `src/core/messages.ts`.
- RPC schemas validate known fields and do not repair them: no `z.coerce`,
  `.default()`, `.transform()`, `.preprocess()`, `.catch()`, or product
  canonicalize. Unknown additive object properties may be stripped by
  `z.object()`. That is forward-compat; content scripts can outlive a
  service-worker update.
- Authorize by sender class after parse: options and popup inbound require an
  extension-page sender (`Unauthorized` or ignore `GET_POPUP_STATE`). Content
  inbound requires `sender.tab.id` or is ignored. Do not match
  `options.html` vs `popup.html`.
- Regular Zod stays out of `content.js`, including `import type` from files
  that runtime-import regular Zod. Content may import `protocol/content/*`
  (Mini only). Overlay, playback, and engine import neither Zod variant.
- `sendContentRequest()` does not runtime-parse requests it constructs;
  only the receiver parses inbound requests. It only runtime-parses the
  unknown response.
- No Zod on `timeupdate`, overlay render, or playback math.

## Media behavior

- Tab-level requested speed and per-video arbitration are separate concerns.
- A single hostile or unusual player must not disable or rewrite state for other videos.
- Preserve one-controller-per-video and lifecycle cleanup guarantees.

## Privacy

- Do not add analytics, telemetry, accounts, remote configuration, remote code,
  or extension-originated network services unless explicitly approved.

## External guidance

- Generic Chrome-extension skills and documentation are advisory.
- Project specifications and repository rules define this extension's intended behavior.
- If platform behavior appears to contradict the current specification, verify the conflict
  and surface it rather than silently redesigning the architecture.